{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observation\nIn the scheduled Windows sweep at `b4e0d01b649` (2026-11-15/16), `tests/unit/test_opcert_privkey_env_pop_932d.py::test_compose_still_works_after_pop` failed in `rebar.opcert_service.keyprov.compose_signer`: `OpcertKeyError: the op-cert key is malformed, encrypted (passphrase-protected), or otherwise unusable: Bad permissions. Try removing permissions for user: \\OWNER RIGHTS (S-1-3-4) on file C:/Users/RUNN...`. That test supplies the key inline, so it never reaches rebar's own mode check; the refusal comes from `ssh-keygen -y` (`src/rebar/opcert_service/keyprov.py:131-151`), which validates the copy `compose_signer` writes into a `tempfile.mkdtemp()` directory (`keyprov.py:67-74`).\n\nWin32-OpenSSH's key permission check accepts only the file owner, SYSTEM and Administrators. The copy's ACL carries an OWNER RIGHTS ACE. Every `compose_signer` path writes the same kind of copy, so once rebar's POSIX mode check is skipped on Windows (almondy-columned-iguanodon) the 30 op-cert tests in `.github/windows-sweep-deselect/openssh-key-acl.args` are expected to stop here.\n\n## Hypotheses (unproven)\n- The OWNER RIGHTS ACE comes from Python 3.13's `os.mkdir(mode=0o700)` on Windows (used by `tempfile.mkdtemp`), which writes a DACL for SYSTEM, Administrators and OWNER RIGHTS, and the key file inherits it.\n- The runner's temp folder ACL contributes the ACE instead.\nThe `icacls` step added to the Windows sweep by almondy-columned-iguanodon prints the ACL of a fresh `mkdtemp` directory and a freshly minted key, which distinguishes these.\n\n## Note on policy\nMaking the copy acceptable to OpenSSH means writing an owner-only DACL on it. On cloistered-decomposed-nymph (2026-11-18) the operator chose option (3), relying on the default ACL, and rejected option (1), an owner-only DACL check plus a protected DACL on key copies. Resolved 2026-11-19 (see comments): option (1) on this ticket reverses that rejection for the temporary copy only, on Windows only; the persistent key file still relies on the default ACL.\n\n## Context / Problem\nOp-cert signing cannot compose on Windows: Win32-OpenSSH refuses the temporary key copy that `compose_signer` writes, because the copy carries an OWNER RIGHTS ACE. The diag run recorded in the comments (run 44581143998) shows the ACE comes from the DACL Python 3.13's `tempfile.mkdtemp` writes on the runtime dir (SYSTEM, Administrators, OWNER RIGHTS; no user entry), and the copy takes that DACL. Operator decision (2026-11-19, option 1, recorded in the comments): on Windows only, rebar writes an owner-only DACL on that temporary copy; POSIX behaviour and the persistent key file's handling (cloistered-decomposed-nymph option 3, default ACL) are unchanged. The 28 op-cert tests in `openssh-key-acl.args` (the Observation's earlier count of 30 predates the file) fail on Windows today for this reason.\n\n## Approach\nIn `src/rebar/opcert_service/keyprov.py`, `compose_signer` gains one Windows-only step, inside its existing cleanup `try`, BEFORE `_write_copy` runs: a new helper `_restrict_dir_to_owner(runtime_dir)` gives the runtime dir an owner-only DACL, so the copy is created under it and is never readable by anyone else at any point. The branch is selected by the module's existing `_POSIX_MODE_BITS` switch (False only where `os.name == \"nt\"`), so POSIX hosts run exactly today's code.\n- The helper reads the current user's SID with `whoami /user /fo csv /nh` (parsed with the stdlib `csv` module), then runs `icacls <dir> /inheritance:r /grant:r *<SID>:(OI)(CI)F`, then `icacls <dir> /remove:g` for OWNER RIGHTS (`*S-1-3-4`), SYSTEM (`*S-1-5-18`) and Administrators (`*S-1-5-32-544`), skipping any of those that equals the current user's SID. Granting before removing means the user never loses access to the dir.\n- Mechanism choice: `icacls` and `whoami` ship with every supported Windows, so no dependency is added; the repo already runs `icacls` in the sweep's ACL probe step, though only to print ACLs, so the ACL-changing flags and the `whoami` CSV format are new uses here and are proven on a real Windows runner before merge (see Testing). The alternative, building the DACL in-process through the Win32 security API over `ctypes` (as CPython does for `mkdir(0o700)`), was rejected: it needs hand-written ctypes structure and handle management in library code with no existing precedent in the repo, against two short documented commands whose effect is checked by `icacls` and by `ssh-keygen` itself. No config key, env var or flag is added.\n- Each `whoami`/`icacls` call runs with a fixed 60-second subprocess timeout (a constant, not configurable); output is captured as bytes and decoded with `errors=\"replace\"`, since the SID itself is ASCII and only the user-name column can carry non-ASCII text, so a non-ASCII account name cannot raise a decode error.\n- Failure disposition: any failure (tool missing, timeout, non-zero exit, unparseable `whoami` output) raises `OpcertKeyError` with a message beginning `cannot restrict the op-cert key copy` (or naming the SID read) so it is distinguishable from the existing key-content rejections. It is fatal and not retried: `compose_signer` runs once at startup, and every existing `OpcertKeyError` already stops startup before any queue, worker or network is created, so an operator fixes the host and restarts. The existing `except BaseException` removes the runtime dir, so no copy is stranded.\n- Portability: the new code runs only on Windows and uses `subprocess.run` with an argument list and `os.path` paths; no POSIX-only API, fd, signal or path-separator assumption.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/opcert_service/keyprov.py` — the Windows-only owner-only DACL step and its helper; module comment updated.\n- `tests/unit/test_opcert_key_copy_windows_acl.py` (new) — the tests below.\n- `.github/windows-sweep-deselect/openssh-key-acl.args` — deleted, so the 28 tests run on Windows again.\n- `.github/workflows/test.yml` — drop the `'@.github/windows-sweep-deselect/openssh-key-acl.args'` argument from the sweep command (`tests/unit/test_windows_sweep_deselect.py` keeps the files and the command in step), and update the ACL probe step's comment so it says the default ACL applies to the persistent key only, while the temporary copy gets an owner-only ACL. The step's name, and so its `mechanism-ok` key, is unchanged.\n- `docs/manifest-signing.md` — state that on Windows rebar gives its temporary signing copy an owner-only ACL, while the persistent key still relies on the default ACL.\n\n## Testing\n- Windows-capability-guarded (skipped unless `os.name == \"nt\"`): `compose_signer` on a freshly generated Ed25519 key succeeds, and `icacls` on the copy lists the current user and none of OWNER RIGHTS, SYSTEM or Administrators.\n- Portable (all platforms, with `_POSIX_MODE_BITS` forced False and `subprocess.run` faked for `whoami`/`icacls`): the `whoami`, grant and remove calls happen in that order and all before the copy is written; every call carries the timeout; the remove list skips the current user's SID; a non-zero `icacls` exit, a timeout, a missing tool, or unparseable `whoami` output raises `OpcertKeyError` and leaves no `rebar-opcert-*` dir behind; `whoami` output with a non-ASCII user name still yields the SID.\n- Portable: with `_POSIX_MODE_BITS` True (POSIX) neither `whoami` nor `icacls` is called.\n- Before merge: a `sweep (windows)` run on a throwaway GitHub branch carrying the fix runs the 28 tests. After merge: a `sweep (windows)` run on main with the deselect file gone, checked at the job level; \"the tests in `openssh-key-acl.args`\" means the 28 node ids that file lists at `91322a91e9e`, and the evidence comment names each one's result in that run.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The source of the OWNER RIGHTS ACE is identified from the sweep's `icacls` output and recorded here.\n- [ ] Either op-cert signing composes and signs on a Windows runner (the tests in `openssh-key-acl.args` pass in a `sweep (windows)` run on main), or the operator's decision to leave it unsupported is recorded and the tests skip on Windows with that reason."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795111694959966169,"uuid":"e6a5b38c-5708-4f55-9b76-1480188bbd00"}